{0 ModelKit XGBoost}

The optional [modelkit-xgboost] package provides weighted CPU histogram-tree
regression and binary/multiclass classification, native missing-value routing, checked input
conversion, immutable specifications, and scoped native interoperability.
Installing [modelkit] does not install this package or its native dependencies.
Trusted fitted-model persistence is available through {!Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact}.
Ordinary estimator helpers attach serialization support for complete fitted
pipelines containing supported preprocessing. Already-fitted regressors can
also become serializable inference pipelines without refitting.

{1 Installation and compatibility}

Install [modelkit-xgboost] separately. The package pins OCaml binding 0.2.1,
requires OCaml 5.2+, and admits native libxgboost major version three. The binding
uses a system libxgboost 3.0+ discovered through pkg-config; CMake, a C++ toolchain,
and OpenMP are native build/runtime dependencies. Install that native library
before installing the OCaml binding, and expose a custom installation's
[lib/pkgconfig] directory through [PKG_CONFIG_PATH] and its shared-library
directory through the platform's runtime loader configuration. Binding 0.2.1's
vendored fallback records static archives in its temporary opam build directory
without installing them, so downstream applications cannot link after opam
removes that directory. A persistent shared-library installation avoids this
packaging defect; rebuilding an already installed binding is necessary when
switching from the fallback. Linux CI builds and installs native 3.0.4 from the
binding's bundled sources before resolving dependencies. [Yojson] is an
adapter-only dependency for bounded native JSON
inspection. Windows is excluded by the binding. Local verification uses
OCaml 5.3.0, binding 0.2.1, and native libxgboost 3.0.4 and 3.4.2 on macOS arm64.
Separate pinned Python references use XGBoost 3.0.4 and 3.4.1. These combinations
are verified; other native releases require their own compatibility evidence.

The tested binding is 0.2.1 throughout. Compatibility evidence is scoped to these
native/compiler/platform combinations:
{ul
 {- Linux x86-64, Ubuntu 24.04, OCaml 5.3.0, native 3.0.4 installed from the
 bundled CPU sources: CI passes production linking, prediction parity
 (with matched-build sampling checks), weighted/grouped CV and search,
 native/pipeline artifact deployment, isolated installed-package inference,
 and conversion/fit/prediction smoke measurements.}
 {- macOS arm64, OCaml 5.3.0, native 3.4.2 from Homebrew: CI passes estimator
 tests, workflows, artifact goldens, odoc examples, package builds,
 isolated installed-package inference, and conversion/fit/prediction smoke
 measurements.}
 {- macOS arm64, OCaml 5.3.0, native 3.0.4: local regression/classification,
 persistence/deployment tests, and conversion/fit/prediction smoke checks pass.}
}
Native major three is an admission boundary, not evidence for every 3.x release.
The portable packages have separate Linux/macOS/Windows CI across OCaml 5.2,
5.3, and 5.5 and require no XGBoost installation. Windows remains outside this
adapter's supported platforms.

On macOS, Homebrew's [xgboost] and [libomp] provide the shared native dependencies.
On Linux, a distribution package older than 3.0 does not satisfy the binding.
The following CPU-only build uses the checksummed binding release's complete
bundled XGBoost 3.0.4 sources, with no Python runtime dependency:

{v
opam source xgboost.0.2.1 --dir=xgboost-binding-source
cmake -S xgboost-binding-source/vendor/xgboost -B xgboost-native-build \
  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$PWD/xgboost-native" \
  -DCMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR=lib -DBUILD_STATIC_LIB=OFF \
  -DUSE_OPENMP=ON -DUSE_CUDA=OFF
cmake --build xgboost-native-build --parallel 2
cmake --install xgboost-native-build
export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$PWD/xgboost-native/lib/pkgconfig${PKG_CONFIG_PATH:+:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH}"
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$PWD/xgboost-native/lib${LD_LIBRARY_PATH:+:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH}"
opam install modelkit-xgboost
v}

Keep this native installation available to the deployed application's linker
and runtime loader. If [xgboost.0.2.1] was already installed using the fallback,
run [opam reinstall xgboost.0.2.1] after setting the discovery paths.

{1 Immutable specifications}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.create} validates histogram-tree parameters.
[clone] preserves the unfitted specification and [params] returns immutable
values; parameter search builders create a fresh specification for each
candidate. Booster creation sets CPU execution, histogram trees, an RNG-derived
seed, and the configured [nthread]. It does not set a training objective or
train a model.

{@ocaml[
let specification =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.create ~rounds:20 ~max_depth:3 ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert ((Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.params specification).Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.threads = 1)
]}

{1 Numeric admission}

{!val:Modelkit_xgboost.Input.features} copies a nonempty ModelKit matrix to
row-major float32 storage and retains its ordered feature schema. Native NaN
means missing; infinity is rejected. Regression targets and weights remain
finite after conversion. Already encoded classification labels must lie in
the range from zero inclusive to [class_count] exclusive and be exactly
representable as float32; learning the mapping from original labels is the
estimator's responsibility.

Float32 conversion is lossy. Each input reports its allocated float32 payload,
full-size temporary staging payload, source shape, and count of changed finite
values. These reports exclude native DMatrix/booster allocations and OCaml
headers. Finite feature/target underflow to zero is permitted and reported;
positive weights rounding to zero fail because that would change which rows
participate. Infinities and finite values rounding to infinity fail with typed
row/column context. Converted buffers are opaque and immutable to callers.

{@ocaml[
let x = Modelkit.Matrix.of_arrays [| [| 0.1; Float.nan |]; [| 2.; 3. |] |]
        |> Result.get_ok
let schema = Modelkit.Feature_schema.of_matrix x |> Result.get_ok
let converted = Modelkit_xgboost.Input.features ~feature_schema:schema x
                |> Result.get_ok
let report = Modelkit_xgboost.Input.feature_report converted
let () = assert (Modelkit_xgboost.Input.payload_bytes report = 16L)
let () = assert (Modelkit_xgboost.Input.rounded_values report = 1)
]}

{1 Fit and predict regression}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor} implements the public {!module-type:Modelkit.REGRESSOR}
protocol. Every fit builds independent native state, runs exactly the configured
number of rounds with squared-error loss, and returns a separate fitted value.
Optional sample weights are routed to the native DMatrix; zero weights remain
zero. Feature NaNs route through native missing-value handling. Training shape,
schema, target/weight alignment, and float32 admission fail with typed errors.

{@ocaml[
let y = Modelkit.Vector.of_array [| 1.; 2. |] |> Modelkit.Target.regression
        |> Result.get_ok
let weights = Modelkit.Sample_weight.of_array ~expected_length:2 [| 1.; 2. |]
              |> Result.get_ok
let fitted =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.fit specification ~sample_weight:weights
    ~rng:(Modelkit.Rng.create (Modelkit.Seed.of_int 41))
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x ~y ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let predictions, conversion =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.predict_with_report fitted ~feature_schema:schema ~x
  |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Modelkit.Target.length predictions = 2)
let () = assert (Modelkit_xgboost.Input.payload_bytes conversion = 16L)
]}

Prediction validates the fitted schema's exact names/order and feature count,
returns finite float64 values in source row order, and supports empty batches
with the fitted column count. {!val:Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.report} exposes native
version, effective seed, rounds, and input conversion reports. Prediction's
conversion report describes only its float32 feature buffer; native output
storage and the returned float64 vector are additional allocations.

Fit detaches the learned model from training caches using an internal
XGBoost-produced model buffer, then releases the training booster and DMatrix.
The fitted value retains no training observations. It owns a GC-managed private
inference booster; native allocations are additional to OCaml heap accounting.
Admission costs linear time and float32 payload in the number of input values.
Cache detachment adds one model-buffer write/read during fit, with temporary
space proportional to model size. Prediction converts its feature batch once
and calls native in-place prediction. Calls sharing a fitted value serialize
native output copying; separate fitted values can predict independently.

Repeated fits/predictions are deterministic for fixed data, parameters, RNG,
native build/platform, and thread configuration. Native sampling changes between
3.0.4 and 3.4.2, so the same seed alone does not reproduce a sampled model across
these versions. Column sampling also uses the C++ runtime's [std::shuffle], whose
sequence can differ between toolchains. Pin the native build and platform when
reproducing sampled fits. Frozen Python sampling references cover macOS arm64;
sampled fits additionally compare to direct upstream calls on the linked native
build, preserving strict prediction/probability tolerances on Linux. The
committed parity fixtures cover dense, weighted, missing/all-missing, sampled,
regularized, large-scale, constant-feature/target, single-row, and zero-learning
rate cases at absolute and relative prediction tolerances of [2e-6].

{1 External-memory regression}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.fit_batches} trains CPU histogram trees from
resettable batches without constructing a complete feature matrix. Supply an
immutable specification and RNG, an ordered named schema, a positive expected
row count, typed [next]/[reset] callbacks, and an existing writable cache
directory. Each batch contains a ModelKit matrix, regression targets, its
schema, and an optional weight vector. Weight vectors allow all-zero batches;
the whole dataset must contain a positive weight. Supply weights consistently
for every batch or omit them consistently.

The source is first validated once, using the ordinary numerical policies:
feature NaNs remain missing, targets are finite, weights are finite/nonnegative,
float32 overflow is rejected, and a positive weight may not round to zero.
Subsequent passes must reproduce the same batch boundaries and row order,
including original feature, target, and weight values. Per-batch BLAKE2b-256
fingerprints compare float64 bits, canonicalizing NaNs, and occupy 32 bytes per
batch in an on-disk replay manifest. The caller owns source replay, selection of
the training scope, and cancellation/job supervision. No automatic streaming
pipeline preprocessing, cross-validation, or fold selection is performed.

This example constructs only one batch at a time and uses the system temporary
directory as the caller-provided cache parent:

{[
let () =
  let open Modelkit in
  let module X = Modelkit_xgboost in
  let names = Feature_names.create ~expected_count:2 [| "signal"; "context" |]
    |> Result.get_ok in
  let schema = Feature_schema.named names in
  let cursor = ref 0 in
  let reset () = cursor := 0; Ok () in
  let next () =
    if !cursor = 4 then Ok None
    else (
      let start = !cursor * 8 in
      incr cursor;
      let x = Matrix.init ~rows:8 ~columns:2 (fun row column ->
        if column = 1 && row mod 3 = 0 then Float.nan
        else float_of_int (start + row + column)) |> Result.get_ok in
      let y = Vector.init ~length:8 (fun row -> float_of_int (start + row))
        |> Result.get_ok |> Target.regression |> Result.get_ok in
      Ok (Some { X.Regressor.feature_schema = schema; x; y;
                 sample_weight = None }))
  in
  let specification = X.Specification.create ~rounds:3 ~max_depth:2 ()
    |> Result.get_ok in
  let fitted = X.Regressor.fit_batches specification
    ~rng:(Rng.create (Seed.of_int 42)) ~feature_schema:schema
    ~expected_rows:32 ~cache_directory:(Filename.get_temp_dir_name ())
    ~next ~reset () |> Result.get_ok in
  let report = X.Regressor.batches_report fitted |> Option.get in
  assert (report.batches = 4 && report.rows = 32);
  assert (report.peak_feature_buffer_bytes = 64L);
  let encoded = X.Artifact.encode_regression fitted |> Result.get_ok in
  let restored = X.Artifact.decode_regression encoded |> Result.get_ok in
  assert (X.Regressor.batches_report restored = Some report)
]}

The adapter owns a unique private child of [cache_directory]. It frees the
training booster, DMatrix, proxy, and callback trampolines before removing that
child's replay manifest and native page files. Success returns the ordinary
{!Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.fitted} type with an independent inference booster;
prediction and supported serialization do not need the source or caches.
If a job terminates the process, its private cache child may remain; reclamation
of abandoned job caches belongs to the caller.
Exceptions in [next]/[reset] become typed callback errors. Native proxy-setter
failures are also contained at the callback boundary. A sticky failure prevents
partial data from being accepted, even if native construction interprets a
failed callback as end-of-input. Cleanup errors are reported, with the fitting
error retained as the primary error if both occur.

Binding 0.2.1's high-level iterator neither contains every proxy-setter exception
nor frees its proxy on explicit DMatrix cleanup. This API therefore uses a small
private bridge to the native iterator/cache entry points and the binding's
low-level training functions. No patched binding or additional upstream release
is needed for this feature. The separate vendored-archive installation issue
described above still applies.

The ordinary regression report aggregates rounded-value counts and converted
payload over the validation pass once. These are dataset totals, not retained
full-size buffers. [batches_report] separately records batch/row/reset/pass
counts, peak float32 feature bytes, peak adapter staging bytes, and replay
fingerprint disk bytes. Peak staging includes float32 features, labels, weights,
and the batch-sized fingerprint buffer. These are active owned payloads, not
process RSS or a GC heap high-water mark. It excludes caller input, OCaml
headers/allocator overhead, array descriptors, digest strings, uncollected
garbage, native allocations, filesystem caches, and the model
snapshot used to detach training state. Tests increase row count tenfold while
holding batch size fixed and check unchanged staging peaks and release of source
and float32 conversion buffers between callbacks.

External memory does not bound total process memory: native labels and weights
are concatenated, and gradients and histograms may scale with the dataset.
Disk I/O and operating-system page caching also affect resource use. See the
{{:https://xgboost.readthedocs.io/en/release_3.0.0/tutorials/external_memory.html}
native external-memory guide}. Our small weighted/unweighted CPU parity fixtures
use full row/column sampling and compare with in-memory fitting at tolerance
[2e-6 + 2e-6 * abs(expected)]. Batch-dependent quantile sketching, sampling, native
versions, and C++ builds can produce different models; the tolerance is fixture
evidence, not a universal equivalence guarantee.

{1 Pipeline integration}

Use {!Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.to_pipeline} for an already-fitted regressor,
including a streamed or previously loaded model. It retains the fitted native
model without refitting, prediction, or an intermediate serialization copy.
The resulting fitted pipeline has no preprocessing: it expects the same ordered
features as the regressor, including any feature engineering performed by the
caller. It is an inference value, not a trainable specification for CV or search.
Original fit provenance and streaming diagnostics survive serialization through
the ordinary {!Modelkit.Artifact} API. Loading still selects a trusted XGBoost
decoder explicitly. No artifact-format migration is required.

{@ocaml[
let wrapped = Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.to_pipeline ~name:"trees" fitted
              |> Result.get_ok
let bytes = Modelkit.Artifact.encode_regression wrapped |> Result.get_ok
let reader = Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.regression_codec ~threads:1 ()
             |> Result.get_ok |> Modelkit.Artifact.Codec.decoder
let restored = Modelkit.Artifact.decode_regression ~decoders:[| reader |] bytes
               |> Result.get_ok |> Modelkit.Artifact.model
let values model =
  Modelkit.Pipeline.predict model ~feature_schema:schema ~x |> Result.get_ok
  |> Modelkit.Target.regression_values |> Modelkit.Vector.to_array
let () = assert (values wrapped = values restored)
]}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.estimator} packages the immutable specification
using public ModelKit modules and reports package/version provenance. It accepts
routed sample weights and works as the terminal estimator of ordinary and
supervised pipelines. Add preprocessing through the usual pipeline APIs.

{@ocaml[
let estimator = Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.estimator ~name:"trees" specification
                |> Result.get_ok
let pipeline = Modelkit.Pipeline.set_estimator Modelkit.Pipeline.empty estimator
               |> Result.get_ok
let fitted_pipeline =
  Modelkit.Pipeline.fit pipeline ~sample_weight:weights
    ~rng:(Modelkit.Rng.create (Modelkit.Seed.of_int 41))
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x ~y ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let predicted = Modelkit.Pipeline.predict fitted_pipeline ~feature_schema:schema ~x
                |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Modelkit.Target.length predicted = 2)
]}

This packaging reports [Pipeline.External_artifact]. The portable core remains
independent of native payloads, and the decoder is selected explicitly when
loading. The internal cache-detachment buffer is separate from the supported
model envelope. Pipelines with unsupported preprocessing fail with typed errors
that identify the component; saving never silently drops preprocessing.

Inspect the fitted pipeline before choosing a deployment path. An attached
external serialization implementation permits saving without making the native
payload portable or safe to load from an untrusted source:

{@ocaml[
let support = Modelkit.Pipeline.artifact_report fitted_pipeline
let terminal = Modelkit.Pipeline.artifact_estimator support
let producer = Option.get terminal.Modelkit.Pipeline.provenance
let () = assert (Modelkit.Pipeline.provenance_package producer = "modelkit-xgboost")
let () = assert (Modelkit.Pipeline.provenance_implementation producer = "Regressor")
let () = assert (terminal.Modelkit.Pipeline.serialization_support = Modelkit.Pipeline.External_artifact)
let () = assert (Modelkit.Pipeline.artifact_supported support)
let () = assert (not (Modelkit.Pipeline.portable_artifact_supported support))
]}

Each transformer has its own component report, so unsupported preprocessing can
be identified before saving. Provenance describes the producing implementation;
it does not itself attach serialization. The standalone model provenance
accessors report adapter/binding versions, original training and model-writer
native versions, current runtime, and inference thread caps.

{@ocaml[
let pipeline_bytes = Modelkit.Artifact.encode_regression fitted_pipeline
                     |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Result.is_error (Modelkit.Artifact.decode_regression pipeline_bytes))
let regression_reader = Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.regression_codec ~threads:1 ()
    |> Result.get_ok |> Modelkit.Artifact.Codec.decoder
let restored_pipeline = Modelkit.Artifact.decode_regression
    ~decoders:[| regression_reader |] pipeline_bytes
    |> Result.get_ok |> Modelkit.Artifact.model
let () = assert (Modelkit.Target.length
    (Modelkit.Pipeline.predict restored_pipeline ~feature_schema:schema ~x
     |> Result.get_ok) = 2)
]}

{1 Binary and multiclass classification}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier} implements the public {!module-type:Modelkit.CLASSIFIER}
protocol using the same immutable specifications as regression. Two effective
classes select binary logistic loss; three or more select softmax cross-entropy.
Fitting maps sorted original integer labels to exact contiguous float32 indices.
The mapping preserves negative labels and labels outside float32's exact integer
range. {!Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.classes} returns a fresh copy of that sorted
vocabulary. Predictions return original labels; probability columns follow
exactly the same order. Exact ties select the lowest label, including binary
probability 0.5.

At least two classes with positive sample weight are required. Empty,
single-class, and single-effective-class targets return typed validation errors.
Zero-weight rows are excluded before native training and do not introduce new
classes or affect histogram cuts. Original feature values still pass the normal
float32/NaN admission policy. Classifier fit reports retain original input shapes
and expose original/effective row counts. If rows are filtered, feature and
weight conversion reports include full-input float32 staging, and the target
report includes label-export and row-selection array payloads. Label-mapping
tables, fitted class storage, native allocations, and model-detachment buffers
are excluded from conversion accounting.

{@ocaml[
let labels = Modelkit.Target.classification [| -7; 16777219 |]
let classifier =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.fit specification ~sample_weight:weights
    ~rng:(Modelkit.Rng.create (Modelkit.Seed.of_int 41))
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x ~y:labels ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let classes = Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.classes classifier
let probabilities =
  Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.predict_proba classifier ~feature_schema:schema ~x
  |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (classes = [| -7; 16777219 |])
let () = assert (Modelkit.Matrix.shape probabilities = (2, 2))
let classifier_estimator = Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.estimator specification
                           |> Result.get_ok
let classification_pipeline =
  Modelkit.Pipeline.set_estimator Modelkit.Pipeline.empty classifier_estimator
  |> Result.get_ok
let classification_model =
  Modelkit.Pipeline.fit classification_pipeline ~sample_weight:weights
    ~rng:(Modelkit.Rng.create (Modelkit.Seed.of_int 41))
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x ~y:labels ()
  |> Result.get_ok
let aligned = Modelkit.Pipeline.predict_proba classification_model
              ~feature_schema:schema ~x |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Modelkit.Matrix.shape aligned = (2, 2))
]}

Binary probability columns are one-minus-positive-probability and positive
probability for the lower/higher labels. Multiclass native probabilities must be
finite, lie between zero and one, and sum to one within [1e-6]; accepted rows
are normalized in float64 to remove float32 rounding. Invalid native output
returns a typed numerical error. Empty prediction batches retain their class
column count. Label-only prediction avoids allocating a full float64 probability
matrix. Classifier prediction shares the regressor's schema, native lifetime,
concurrency, and version-dependent reproducibility contracts. Mapping takes
linear time in rows plus class sorting; filtering adds linear staging in input
size, and multiclass prediction scales with rows times classes.

The estimator helper publishes sample-weight, class, and probability dispatch
with package/version provenance. Raw decision margins are not exposed. Pipeline
serialization is attached automatically. Select
{!Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.classification_codec} explicitly when loading.
Pinned classifier references cover binary and multiclass dense,
weighted, missing/all-missing, sampled, regularized, constant-feature,
imbalanced, and zero-weight-class cases at absolute/relative [2e-6] tolerance.

{1 Persist and restore fitted models}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact} provides task-specific byte and file APIs. Regression
readers restore {!Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.fitted}; classification readers
restore {!Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.fitted} for binary or multiclass models.
Loading uses no observations, training pipeline, refitting, Python, or R.

{@ocaml[
let regression_bytes = Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.encode_regression fitted
                       |> Result.get_ok
let restored_regression = Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.decode_regression
    ~threads:1 regression_bytes |> Result.get_ok
let restored_predictions = Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.predict restored_regression
    ~feature_schema:schema ~x |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Modelkit.Target.length restored_predictions = 2)
let class_bytes = Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.encode_classification classifier
                  |> Result.get_ok
let restored_classifier = Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.decode_classification
    ~threads:1 class_bytes |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.classes restored_classifier = classes)
let () = assert (Modelkit.Matrix.shape
    (Modelkit_xgboost.Classifier.predict_proba restored_classifier
       ~feature_schema:schema ~x |> Result.get_ok) = (2, 2))
let path = Filename.temp_file "modelkit-xgboost-model-" ".bin"
let () = Fun.protect ~finally:(fun () -> Sys.remove path) (fun () ->
    Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.save_regression ~path fitted |> Result.get_ok;
    let restored = Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.load_regression ~threads:1 ~path ()
                   |> Result.get_ok in
    assert (Modelkit.Target.length
      (Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.predict restored ~feature_schema:schema ~x
       |> Result.get_ok) = 2))
]}

The model envelope contains package-qualified codec identity,
original fit and current writer/binding/native provenance, all immutable
specification parameters, exact feature schema, original class mapping,
conversion reports and seed, float32 input/float64 output policy, native-NaN
missing semantics, CPU histogram scope, and the full prediction range.
XGBoost-produced JSON stores the trees and objective. Native memory snapshots
and training-configuration dumps are excluded; configuration is inspected only
after loading and is never restored as training state.

Ordinary fits retain the version-one payload. External-memory regression adds
batch diagnostics in payload version two; the current reader accepts both.
Existing artifacts do not acquire invented streaming diagnostics when loaded.

Inference [~threads] defaults to one and leaves original training parameters
unchanged. The loaded booster owns no training cache or model-buffer reference.
Serialization and prediction share a fitted-value lock, so saving cannot
invalidate concurrent inference. Fit diagnostics retain the producing process's
allocation counts and word size. Provenance accessors distinguish training,
model-JSON, and runtime versions; resaving emits JSON with the current runtime
version while preserving original fit provenance.

Load only trusted artifacts from this supported writer. Upstream warns that
arbitrary externally authored model JSON can cause undefined behavior or crashes.
The envelope's MD5 checksum detects accidental corruption and does not
authenticate a model. Bounds and consistency checks improve diagnostics; they
do not extend the portable core's safe-reader guarantee to native JSON.
See {{:https://xgboost.readthedocs.io/en/stable/tutorials/saving_model.html}XGBoost's
model IO contract} for the native trust and backward-compatibility boundaries.

Before native loading, the reader validates complete framing/checksum, lengths,
versions, policies, immutable parameters, schema fingerprints, reports, class
mapping, native model metadata, objective, feature/output dimensions, and tree
range. After loading it checks native feature/round counts and current
objective/output/CPU/thread configuration before returning fitted state.
Acquired native resources are released on every failed validation or exception.
The reader accepts binding provenance 0.2.1 and native major three. Its runtime
must be at least as new as the JSON writer: older-model loading into a newer
supported runtime follows the stable-model direction, while forward loading is
rejected. Version combinations still need explicit compatibility evidence.

Default limits are 64 MiB bytes, one million features/classes, one MiB strings,
and a fixed maximum JSON nesting depth of 64. JSON inspection allocates temporary
OCaml memory linear in model size; native allocation remains additional. Smaller
byte limits also constrain temporary configuration inspection. Save functions
encode fully before opening the destination, then write directly; callers own
atomic replacement, authentication, and transport.

The codec factories expose {!Modelkit.Artifact.Codec} attachments and explicit
reader selections. Their decoder checks the outer pipeline component's producer
identity, schema, and class mapping against the native envelope before loading.
The ordinary regression/classification estimator helpers attach serialization
automatically. Use the core's task-specific pipeline byte/file APIs to retain
supported preprocessing. A complete pipeline's input schema describes the raw
features; its output feature schema describes the native estimator's transformed
input. Both are preserved and checked during loading.

Committed native-model and fitted-pipeline goldens cover regression, binary,
and multiclass tasks with imputation, weighted scaling, and variance filtering.
Fresh-process inference receives artifacts and new probe rows only, with no
training data or refitting. Native 3.0.4 artifacts are verified on 3.0.4 and
3.4.2; 3.4.2 artifacts are verified on 3.4.2 and rejected on 3.0.4 as forward
loads. Prediction/probability agreement uses absolute plus relative [2e-6].
An isolated consumer built from installed runtime packages uses only ModelKit,
this adapter, and declared runtime dependencies, without test libraries or
private source modules.

{1 Cross-validation and parameter search}

The estimator helpers work with the public regression, binary-classification,
and multiclass-classification CV/search APIs. Keep preprocessing inside the
pipeline builder: each candidate and fold learns its own preprocessing and
native model from its training rows. Dataset weights are routed to estimator
fitting and weighted scoring; preprocessing follows each stage's metadata
requests. Group-aware splitters keep groups out of opposing partitions.
Every training fold must contain at least two positively weighted classes for
classification. [Cross_validation.Record] retains a typed fitting failure and
successful peer folds when that condition is absent.

This regression recipe uses mean imputation, group exclusion, two weighted
metrics, and a full-data refit selected by negative mean squared error.

{@ocaml[
open Modelkit
let search_x = Matrix.init ~rows:12 ~columns:2 (fun row column ->
    if column = 0 then float_of_int row
    else if row mod 3 = 0 then Float.nan else float_of_int (row mod 5))
    |> Result.get_ok
let search_y = Vector.of_array (Array.init 12 (fun row -> float_of_int (row * row)))
    |> Target.regression |> Result.get_ok
let search_weights = Sample_weight.of_array ~expected_length:12
    (Array.init 12 (fun row -> 1. +. float_of_int (row mod 2))) |> Result.get_ok
let search_groups = Groups.create ~expected_length:12
    (Array.init 12 (fun row -> row / 3)) |> Result.get_ok
let search_data = Dataset.create ~finiteness:Dataset.Allow_nan
    ~sample_weight:search_weights ~groups:search_groups
    ~x:search_x ~y:search_y () |> Result.get_ok
let build_trees depth =
  let specification = Modelkit_xgboost.Specification.create
      ~rounds:3 ~max_depth:depth ~threads:1 () |> Result.get_ok in
  let imputer = Modelkit.Artifact.simple_imputer_stage ~name:"impute"
      (Simple_imputer.mean ()) |> Result.get_ok in
  let builder = Pipeline.add_transformer Pipeline.empty imputer |> Result.get_ok in
  let estimator = Modelkit_xgboost.Regressor.estimator specification |> Result.get_ok in
  Pipeline.set_estimator builder estimator
let search_splitter = Group_k_fold.create ~folds:2 () |> Result.get_ok
    |> Cross_validation.target_independent_splitter (module Group_k_fold)
let search_scorers = [| Regression_scorer.neg_mean_squared_error;
                       Regression_scorer.neg_mean_absolute_error |]
let search_seed = Seed.of_int 43
let cv = Cross_validation.Regression.cross_validate
    ~splitter:search_splitter ~scorers:search_scorers ~seed:search_seed
    (build_trees 2 |> Result.get_ok) search_data |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Cross_validation.successful_fold_count cv = 2)
let depth_axis = Grid_search.axis ~name:"max_depth" ~values:[| 1; 2 |]
    ~encode:(fun value -> Grid_search.Int value) ~set:(fun _ value -> Ok value)
    |> Result.get_ok
let tree_grid = Grid_search.create ~base:1 ~build:build_trees [| depth_axis |]
    |> Result.get_ok
let search = Grid_search.Regression.search ~grid:tree_grid
    ~splitter:search_splitter ~scorers:search_scorers ~refit:"neg_mean_squared_error"
    ~seed:search_seed search_data |> Result.get_ok
let selected = Grid_search.selection search |> Result.get_ok
let predictions = Pipeline.predict selected.Grid_search.selected_model
    ~feature_schema:(Dataset.feature_schema search_data) ~x:search_x |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Target.length predictions = 12)
]}

The selected model is the full-data refit, including its learned preprocessing.
Save the complete fitted pipeline and explicitly select native deserialization
when deploying it; no training dataset is needed for loading or prediction.

{@ocaml[
let deployment_path = Filename.temp_file "modelkit-xgboost-pipeline-" ".bin"
let () = Fun.protect ~finally:(fun () -> Sys.remove deployment_path) (fun () ->
    Modelkit.Artifact.save_regression ~path:deployment_path
      selected.Grid_search.selected_model |> Result.get_ok;
    let decoder = Modelkit_xgboost.Artifact.regression_codec ~threads:1 ()
      |> Result.get_ok |> Modelkit.Artifact.Codec.decoder in
    let deployed = Modelkit.Artifact.load_regression ~decoders:[| decoder |]
      ~path:deployment_path () |> Result.get_ok |> Modelkit.Artifact.model in
    let served = Modelkit.Pipeline.predict deployed
      ~feature_schema:(Dataset.feature_schema search_data) ~x:search_x
      |> Result.get_ok in
    assert (Target.length served = 12);
    assert (Feature_schema.equal (Pipeline.input_schema deployed)
      (Dataset.feature_schema search_data)))
]}

Randomized search takes the same builder and splitter. A finite choice space
visits distinct combinations without replacement.

{@ocaml[
let sampled_axis = Randomized_search.axis ~name:"max_depth"
    ~distribution:(Parameter_distribution.choice [| 1; 2 |] |> Result.get_ok)
    ~encode:(fun value -> Grid_search.Int value) ~set:(fun _ value -> Ok value)
    |> Result.get_ok
let tree_space = Randomized_search.create ~iterations:2 ~base:1
    ~build:build_trees [| sampled_axis |] |> Result.get_ok
let randomized = Randomized_search.Regression.search ~space:tree_space
    ~splitter:search_splitter ~scorers:search_scorers ~refit:"neg_mean_squared_error"
    ~seed:search_seed search_data |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Array.length (Grid_search.candidates randomized) = 2)
]}

Successive halving budgets training rows, keeps validation rows fixed, and
performs fresh fits on nested training subsets. [rounds] remains an immutable
estimator parameter; halving does not continue a fitted booster or budget tree
rounds. Its selected pipeline is refitted on the complete dataset.

{@ocaml[
let row_budget = Successive_halving.budget ~min_samples:3 ~max_samples:6
    ~factor:2 ~max_fits:7 () |> Result.get_ok
let halved = Successive_halving.Regression.search
    ~candidates:(Successive_halving.of_grid tree_grid) ~budget:row_budget
    ~splitter:search_splitter ~scorers:search_scorers ~refit:"neg_mean_squared_error"
    ~seed:search_seed search_data |> Result.get_ok
let () = assert (Array.length (Successive_halving.rounds halved) = 2)
let _selected = Successive_halving.selection halved |> Result.get_ok
]}

Pass [~execution:(Modelkit_parallel.execution backend)] to these APIs to use
the optional [modelkit-parallel] package. Configure each specification with
[~threads:1], create the backend with [~inner_threads:1], and start the process
with both [OMP_NUM_THREADS=1] and [OMP_THREAD_LIMIT=1]. Search visits candidates
in stable order and parallelizes their folds. Integration tests verify identical
scores, selection, class order, predictions, and probabilities across sequential
and 1-, 2-, and 4-domain execution for all three tasks on the tested runtimes.

{1 Conversion and resource evidence}

Development smoke measurements cover feature/target/weight conversion and
weighted regression, binary, and multiclass fit/prediction. The fixed workload
has 4,096 rows, 16 features, missing values, zero weights, 12 rounds, and 64
prediction rows. Each operation has one warmup and three measured repetitions;
checks require identical repeated-fit responses, finite outputs, exact reported
conversion payload, original class labels, and normalized probabilities.

Timings exclude process startup, source generation, GC preparation, and response
checks. Fit includes admission, native training, and training-cache detachment.
Prediction includes float32 feature conversion and copying outputs, returning
regression values or classification probabilities. OCaml allocation counters and
numeric buffer payload are reported separately; native allocations and peak
resident memory are unmeasured. These are noncomparative smoke measurements,
with no speed claim or machine-dependent pass/fail threshold.

Versioned raw local reports and reproduction commands live in
[dev/benchmarks/README.md]. Native CI runs the same worker and retains its JSON
report for each platform. This development tooling is not installed with the
library and needs no Python runtime in deployed inference applications.

{1 Native interoperability and lifetime}

{!Modelkit_xgboost.Native.with_dmatrix} validates target/weight alignment before
allocating native state, creates a fresh DMatrix, and releases it when the
callback exits. {!Modelkit_xgboost.Native.with_booster} similarly scopes a new
configured booster. Nest the booster scope inside its training DMatrix scope.
Callbacks borrow upstream handles: do not free them, retain them, or use them
after the scope ends. Binding failures become {!Modelkit.Error.t}; unrelated
callback exceptions propagate after cleanup. Returning a typed callback error
also releases both resources.

Booster thread limits do not constrain DMatrix construction. Binding 0.2.1 has
no constructor thread-limit argument. For bounded native concurrency, configure
OpenMP before starting the process with both [OMP_NUM_THREADS=1] and
[OMP_THREAD_LIMIT=1] when parallel folds each use one native thread. This package
does not mutate process-global settings. [modelkit-parallel]'s [inner_threads]
value is diagnostic and does not configure OpenMP or XGBoost.

The single-thread startup contract bounds DMatrix construction as well as
booster training and prediction. Tests inspect the loaded OpenMP runtime's
maximum thread count and thread limit on the caller and worker domains before
and after native operations; fresh-process tests accept the one-thread profile
and reject uncapped/two-thread profiles. These probes are test-only. The adapter
does not enforce or change the process's OpenMP configuration at runtime.
